# SQL & TimescaleDB Conventions

## Client and migrations
- `pg` driver via a NestJS module wrapping a single pool per service.
- Migrations: `node-pg-migrate` or Knex. Versioned, forward-only, reviewed in PR.
- One migration file per change. Never edit a committed migration — write a new one.
- Migrations run on deploy via a separate one-shot job, not on app boot.

## Connection pooling
- Pool size = `(2 * cpu_count) + effective_disk_count`, tuned per service.
- Statement timeout: 5s for OLTP queries, 30s for analytics (separate pool if needed).
- `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`: 10s. Catches forgotten transactions.
- Health check uses a cheap `SELECT 1`, not a real query.

## Schema design (telemetry)
- TimescaleDB hypertable on `telemetry`:
  ```sql
  CREATE TABLE telemetry (
    device_id   UUID NOT NULL,
    ts          TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    lat         DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL,
    lng         DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL,
    battery     SMALLINT,
    raw         JSONB,
    PRIMARY KEY (device_id, ts)
  );
  SELECT create_hypertable('telemetry', 'ts', chunk_time_interval => INTERVAL '1 day');
  ```
- Composite primary key `(device_id, ts)` for upsert idempotency and locality.
- Index on `(device_id, ts DESC)` for "last N readings" queries.
- Compression policy: compress chunks older than 7 days (`ALTER TABLE ... SET (timescaledb.compress, ...)`).
- Retention policy: drop chunks older than 90 days unless overridden.

## Schema design (devices)
- Vanilla Postgres tables. Foreign keys for referential integrity.
- UUIDs (v7 if available, v4 fallback) for primary keys. Never auto-increment for distributed-friendly IDs.
- `created_at` and `updated_at` on every table, default `NOW()`, updated via trigger.
- Soft delete only when audit requires it; otherwise hard delete with audit log.

## Query patterns
- Parameterised queries always. No string interpolation. ORM or query builder mandatory.
- Avoid `SELECT *`. Name columns explicitly so schema evolution is safe.
- For TimescaleDB time-range queries, always include the time column in WHERE — chunk pruning depends on it.
- Use `time_bucket()` for aggregations: `SELECT time_bucket('1 minute', ts), avg(battery) FROM telemetry WHERE ...`.
- Continuous aggregates for dashboard rollups (1-min, 5-min, 1-hour buckets).

## Transactions
- One transaction per logical operation. No transactions spanning HTTP requests.
- `READ COMMITTED` default. `REPEATABLE READ` only when correctness demands it (rare).
- Outbox writes use the same transaction as the domain write — non-negotiable.
- No long-running transactions. If it takes >1s, redesign.

## Performance
- `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` every query in development before it ships.
- Index for the queries actually run, not speculatively. Monitor `pg_stat_user_indexes` for unused indexes.
- Prepared statements via the driver — `pg` handles this automatically with named queries.
- Bulk inserts: `INSERT ... VALUES (...), (...), ...` or `COPY` for large batches. Never row-by-row in a loop.
- `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` for idempotent ingestion: `INSERT INTO telemetry (...) VALUES (...) ON CONFLICT (device_id, ts) DO NOTHING`.

## TimescaleDB specifics
- Hypertable inserts auto-route to the right chunk; nothing special required in app code.
- Don't update old chunks if compression is enabled — decompression cost is real.
- Continuous aggregates refresh on a schedule (`add_continuous_aggregate_policy`); dashboards read from these, not raw.
- Check `timescaledb_information.chunks` for chunk health in ops dashboards.

## Repository pattern
- One repository class per aggregate (Device, Fence, Telemetry).
- Methods return domain objects, never raw rows.
- No business logic — only data access.
- Repository interface in `domain/`, implementation in `infrastructure/`.

## Anti-patterns to refuse
- ORMs that obscure SQL for hot paths (TypeORM is acceptable for CRUD; use raw SQL for telemetry queries).
- N+1 queries — eager load or batch.
- Cross-service joins. Each service owns its data.
- Foreign keys across service boundaries.
- Triggers for business logic (use them only for `updated_at` and audit).
- Long-running migrations on hot tables without `CONCURRENTLY` indexes.
